{{Short description|Portrayal of Baliscans in different cultures}}
{{Culture of Balisca}}
'''Stereotypes of Baliscans''' are generalizations about [[Baliscans]] that may or may not reflect reality. Stereotypes associated with Baliscans vary from country to country depending on the prevalent stereotype in each culture, but are typically rooted in aspects of [[Balisca#Culture and society|Baliscan culture]].

In [[Argentina]], [[Brazil]] and [[W:Uruguay|Uruguay]], Baliscans are often stereotyped as [[Wikt:arrogant|arrogant]] and excessively [[Wiktionary:Proud|proud]]. In addition to the above, Baliscans have also been labeled as lazy, vain, and pedantic, but also kind, generous and carefree, in scientific polls. In some Spanish-speaking countries (such as [[W:Spain|Spain]], [[W:Colombia|Colombia]], [[W:Paraguay|Paraguay]] and [[W:Peru|Peru]]), Baliscan are stereotyped as passionate –though sometimes excessively– as well as noble, honest, and kind.

